From 3d552811a2a3c38e48fd8adf522c91643f494bc9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Pandemonium Dev <dev@pandemonium.local>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 19:40:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 02/10] feat(engine,client): render the fog of war through the
 player's own view
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The client drew the full snapshot — every entity always visible, no fog
visualization at all, while the simulation maintained a full three-state
fog the presentation never consumed. A10 proves the sim's fog integrity;
the human's window never showed it.

Two halves:

- engine: MatchHost::alpha() exposes the clock's interpolation fraction so
  the client can blend its own view snapshot at the exact point the host's
  full-snapshot path uses.
- client: the app keeps a view interpolator over the fog-filtered
  PlayerView (entities AND fog tiles). Every drawn pixel and every screen
  query — click select, box select, context orders, control-group recall —
  now reads through it, so hidden enemies cannot be clicked either (the
  gate's NotVisible rule and the picker finally agree). The fog decal is a
  per-tile alpha texture (hidden/explored/visible) drawn over the terrain
  geometry itself — same vertices, same depth — with linear sampling for
  soft fog edges. The texture refreshes once per sim step, not per frame.

The embedded WGSL now machine-parses in a unit test through wgpu's naga
re-export: a shader typo used to surface only as a runtime panic on a
machine with a display, which headless CI could never catch.
