From 3176436ae7a5f2596861e7117932c12af881774f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Pandemonium Dev <dev@pandemonium.local>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 19:52:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 03/10] feat(engine,client): directional lighting, shaded
 terrain, per-kind silhouettes
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The review's visual verdict: every entity was the same unlit team-colored
cube on flat two-color ground — nothing read as a game. This pass keeps
the placeholder-art law (primitive boxes, no assets) but shades and
shapes what it draws:

- The whole scene gains a fixed sun: terrain and entity shaders now take
  per-quad / per-face normals and Lambert-shade (ambient + diffuse).
- Terrain albedo upgrades from two flat colors to a shaded palette: dry
  steppe grass vs gray-brown rock with per-tile deterministic variation
  (a coordinate hash — same map shades identically every run), a subtle
  two-tile checker for flat-area grain, and a rocky tint by heightmap
  elevation. Terrain vertices now carry the quad normal (flat-shaded,
  computed from the heightmap ramp).
- Entities become per-kind silhouettes composed from 1-3 boxes, derived
  capability-shaped from the loaded content (never name-matched):
  Resource -> amber crystal cluster, Attack+Footprint (no Move) ->
  turret slab + barrel, Footprint -> footprint-sized building slab +
  roof, Move -> body+head unit facing its movement direction with the
  moving-squat cue kept. Per-kind deterministic size variation lets
  infantry kinds read apart. Selection/hit brightening still wins.
- The instance builder is a pure, unit-tested function (build_instances):
  silhouette composition, facing yaw, the moving squat, node color, and
  whole-silhouette capacity trimming are all pinned.
- The embedded-shader parse test earned its keep immediately: it caught
  the WGSL module-level 'let' mistake this pass first wrote.
