From 5ffff44ea8885c858ed18dfc422ed56a00302654 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Pandemonium Dev <dev@pandemonium.local>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 20:11:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 06/10] feat(client): the command card - train, build
 placement, queue cancel, rally
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The review's central finding: the human could move, gather, and attack,
but could not train a unit, raise a structure, cancel production, or set
a rally - the entire economy half of the minimum viable loop was
AI-only. This commit gives the human the same reach the AI has, through
the same command gate (FD-2):

- A bottom bar (plan §11.4): selection panel (name + health for a single
  pick, per-kind census for a group, and a controls reminder when
  nothing is selected), a command card, and a production queue strip.
- The command card offers the selected producer's faction train list
  (name + ore cost, dimmed when unaffordable or when a requirement is
  not yet visible) and, for a selected worker, a build button for every
  roster structure. Pressing builds arms plan §11.3's placement mode.
- Placement mode: a footprint-sized ghost follows the cursor, tinted
  green/red by a client-side legality preview (bounds, buildable terrain,
  visible static bodies - the gate re-checks everything including
  movers); left-click places, right-click or Esc cancels.
- The queue strip shows the selected producer's items with the head
  item's progress percent and a per-item cancel button (CancelQueueItem).
- Right-clicking ground with a single producer selected sets its rally
  (SetRally) instead of a move order a building could never obey.
- Buttons emit client intent through submit_order - one gate, one
  refusal path; the new ui module is pure and unit-tested (layout
  bounds, train/build/cancel button emission, affordability dimming,
  placement legality, kind-index resolution).
